  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 229 PM EDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nAs the weekend progresses, the mid to upper level trough will\nsettle deeper into the region as a low tries to develop at the\nsurface to the east of the Bahamas. This will reinforce the\nnortheasterly wind flow pattern which will in turn cause the\npotential for a strong wind field to develop over the Atlantic\nwaters and potentially spread across the peninsula. Portions of\nthe east coast could encounter wind gusts in excess of 25-30 mph \non Sunday, Monday, and possibly Tuesday which will need to be \nmonitored in case a Wind Advisory were to become necessary.\n\nAs the mid level low develops, the increasing convective activity\nthat may be enabled could be slow-moving in a moist environment.\nWPC keeps the east coast metro areas in a Slight risk for flash\nflooding from excessive rainfall on Sunday with a Marginal risk\nflanking the area on Saturday and Monday as of this afternoon.\nPrecipitable water values of 1.5 to 2.0 inches which runs in the\nrealm of 75 to 99th percentile in the sounding climatology for\nlate October in South Florida.\n\nThe mid-level low begins to retreat northward Wednesday into\nThursday, but the surface feature remains over and to the north of\nthe central Bahamas. High pressure both aloft over the eastern\nseaboard and at the surface from the Gulf will try to build in\nlater in the week which could potentially help lower the coverage\nof convection but the persistently easterly flow component will\nkeep mentionable rain chances in the forecast through the period\nalong the east coast.",
